-- title:
--   Existence of a divisorial Weil pairing datum at level n
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and let $F$ be a field which is a $K$-algebra, subject to the hypothesis `hfg` that some $x \in F$ is transcendental over $K$ and $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Assume `IsCurveOver K F`, i.e. every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor of degree zero recording the orders $v(f)$ at all places $v$ (a place being a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, distinct from $F$ itself, whose valuation ring is a principal ideal ring), each residue field of a place is finite over $K$, and $\Omega_{F/K}$ is free of rank one over $F$. Let $n$ be a nonzero natural number. Then the type `DivisorialWeilPairingData K F n` is nonempty: there exists a map $e \colon \mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)[n] \times \mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)[n] \to K$ on the $n$-torsion of the group of degree-zero divisor classes such that (i) for every Weil datum of level $n$ — divisors $D_1, D_2$ with disjoint supports consisting of rational places, together with nonzero $f_1, f_2 \in F$ satisfying $v(f_i) = n\,D_i(v)$ for all $v$ — the value of $e$ on the classes of $D_1$ and $D_2$ equals the datum's pairing $f_1(D_2)/f_2(D_1)$; and (ii) a moving clause holds: for every $n$-torsion class $x$ and every finite set $S$ of places there is a degree-zero divisor representing $x$ whose support consists of rational places (places whose residue field is generated by the image of $K$) avoiding $S$.
--
--   This is the existence, at every nonzero level $n$, of the Weil pairing on the $n$-torsion of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of a curve over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, packaged divisorially together with the moving lemma it needs. It is the source of the pairing used downstream, for instance by [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.exists_weilPairing`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.exists_weilPairing) and by the construction of the Weil pairing on Tate modules.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Pic0_nonempty_divisorialWeilPairingData.lean

